WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:37.000
This is Hidden Brain. I'm Shankar Vedantam. In his great novel, Anna Karenina, Leo Tolstoy spends hundreds of pages describing Count Vronsky's desperate longing to be with Anna, the woman he loves. He risks his reputation, his military career, his standing in society. He endures uncertainty, jealousy, torment. And finally, after all the struggle and sacrifice, he gets what he wants. Anna is his. But almost immediately, something strange happens.

2
00:00:37.000 --> 00:01:11.000
The feeling he imagined would satisfy him forever begins to fade. Tolstoy writes, in spite of the complete fulfillment of what he had so long desired, he was not completely happy. He soon felt that the realization of his longing gave him only one grain of the mountain of bliss he had anticipated. That realization showed him the eternal error men make by imagining that happiness consists in the gratification of their wishes.

3
00:01:11.000 --> 00:01:51.000
Many of us make what Tolstoy called the eternal error. We assume that external circumstances, a better job, more money, the right relationship, will finally make us happy. Or we blame our unhappiness on what's happening around us and the people in our lives. But psychologists increasingly believe that the greatest obstacles to happiness are not. not in external events or the people we encounter. The barriers to happiness lie within us. This week on Hidden Brain, understanding the invisible emotional machinery

4
00:01:51.000 --> 00:02:29.000
that keeps lasting fulfillment just out of reach. Most of us think of happiness as something the world gives or withholds. If only, I had more money. If only, I found the right partner. If only, I were able to move to a different city. But what if many of the four people? that erode happiness emerge from within, from our own emotional makeup.

5
00:02:29.000 --> 00:02:49.000
At Carnegie Mellon University, psychologist George Lowenstein studies these internal saboteurs of happiness. George Lowenstein, welcome to Hidden Brain. Thank you for having me on the show. George, at the very start of your career as a professor, you were finishing up your graduate studies and going on the job market.

6
00:02:49.000 --> 00:03:46.000
I understand that your classmates had 10 or 20 job interviews lined up. interviews lined up. Did you as well? I didn't. My degree is actually in economics, and I went to the economics meetings, which is where people search for a job, and I had no interviews at all, except I had one with a company that wanted to hire me as a brand manager to sell dog food. Okay. So after this inauspicious start, you ended up last. something of a dream job. Tell me what happened, George. It was really a fluke. I did get a interview at the University of Chicago, and I went there and I managed to really blow it. My colleagues there later told me that

7
00:03:46.000 --> 00:04:39.000
they had voted not to hire me. But I did have a really fantastic dinner the night before the interview with some senior faculty, including the head of the group named Hilly Einhorn. And he liked the dinner so much that he offered me a job over the vote of the group. So I just clawed my way into an academic position. You must have been ecstatic. I mean, given the fact that you had relatively few prospects to now land at the University of Chicago, you must have been ecstatic. Did you stay ecstatic, George? I was probably more relieved than ecstatic, but I was happy about it for a few weeks.

8
00:04:39.000 --> 00:05:21.000
And my wife and I moved to Chicago, and then I got a whole lot less happy. They put me in probably the worst office in the building. It was in the basement. It had these slit winters. and it was located directly under the Dean's bathroom. And there was a huge pipe that came down through the ceiling and then went off to the side. And every time the Dean went to the toilet, I could hear the toilet

9
00:05:21.000 --> 00:06:15.000
flushing through this pipe. So it was a pretty miserable a pretty miserable beginning. And also, I suddenly realized that I had this new hurdle, and that was to get promoted and to get tenure. And actually, that was one of the most miserable years of my life, the first year at the University of Chicago. So psychologists have a name for the phenomenon you've experienced, the hedonic treadmill. We've talked about this idea many times on Hidden Brain, but in many ways I feel it doesn't quite capture the scale of the phenomenon. What is the hedonic treadmill, George? The hedonic treadmill is the

10
00:06:15.000 --> 00:07:16.000
idea that we keep thinking that the next accomplishment, the next goal that we satisfy, the next material object that we crave, if we only get that, if we only accomplish our goal, that we will finally achieve the happiness that has been elusive so far. But, of course, as soon as we do achieve these goals or obtain these material things, they recede into the background and new goals emerge, new wants. And so we're on a treadmill where we always end up in the same place. So our capacity to adapt to experiences includes intense experiences. And I understand that at one point you went canoeing with a friend, Peter Eubel,

11
00:07:16.000 --> 00:07:42.000
who's also been a former guest on Hidden Rain. I'm wondering if you can paint a picture for me of the day and what happened, George. Well, it was in the early 1990s, and it was in the winter, and it rained for several days in a row. In fact, it poured down rain for several days in a row, and I realized that rivers that were ordinarily not navigable

12
00:07:42.000 --> 00:08:15.000
would become navigable. and I located a river. It was a tributary of the Cheat River in West Virginia. And Peter and I got up very early, put the canoe on the car, put the bikes on the car. And we drove to this river. We dropped the bikes off at the bottom of the river, put the canoe in at the top. And sure enough, it was an extremely exhilarating day. the rapids were very dramatic.

13
00:08:15.000 --> 00:08:40.000
We kept on swamping the canoe and having to pull it over to the shore and bail it out, then get back on the river, and it was a lot of excitement. And then at some point, the river broadened out, and the rapids ended, and we were just floating calmly down the river.

14
00:08:40.000 --> 00:09:03.000
And I think we were both, experiencing a tremendous letdown. So in other words, you'd enjoy the thrills of the river, and in some ways the hedonic treadman had kicked in at this point, and you're saying, all right, those were fun, but now we're in calm, clear water. It's kind of boring. That's right.

15
00:09:03.000 --> 00:09:49.000
Yeah, we very rapidly habituated to the excitement and to the danger, and we felt let down, and we felt bored. We were canoeing along, and I noticed up ahead, a dam that the river went over a dam, and we pulled the canoe over, and I went ahead, and I looked at the dam, and there was millions of gallons of water flowing over the dam. The dam was about three feet high, and I said to Peter, let's shoot the dam, like basically let's ride the canoe over the dam, which was a completely ridiculous idea. And Peter, had very little outdoor experience,

16
00:09:49.000 --> 00:10:32.000
so he wasn't in a position to judge what a bad idea it was. So I got back in the canoe, and we were canoeing at top speed as fast as we could, right over the dam, which was completely ridiculous because as soon as the nose got too far over, the canoe just did a nose dive into the turbulence at the bottom of the dam, and we got trapped in what's called a hydraulic. When water pours over a dam or a waterfall, it pierces the surface of the water below,

17
00:10:32.000 --> 00:11:28.000
and it creates a kind of a water vacuum, and the upper surface of the river flows up. It flows upstream. And we got trapped in the hydraulic, and the canoe was like spinning on its axis like a water wheel. And it was kind of crazy, crazy conditions, millions of gallons of water flowing over the dam right on top of us. And at some point, Peter started waving his arms like somebody who's drowning, which in effect he was. And I tried to kick him out of the hydraulic, but I failed. And he just floated past me to the place where the biggest volume of water was coming down over the water. the dam and I could see that he got driven under water by the water coming over the dam and he

18
00:11:28.000 --> 00:12:37.000
disappeared from view. That was almost unquestionably the worst moment of my life and I didn't see him re-emerge and I knew his wife. I'd met his parents and I was treading water and the water was pouring down on me. I was getting colder and colder. We weren't wearing wetsuits. It was in the middle of winter. Finally, I realized that I was going to die too. And part of me was actually relieved. In the end, I end up floating exactly to the place that he had floated to. And there was so much force of water that it drove me underwater. And I remember looking up, seeing the gray sky, and thinking this is the last light you'll ever see. I also thought to myself, my son at the time was two years old,

19
00:12:37.000 --> 00:13:11.000
and I thought I'll never see my son again, and he'll grow up without a father. And so it was a very sad feeling. A few seconds later, I popped out downstream, and my immediate reaction was, oh, I'm alive. alive, but life isn't going to be worth living because I killed my friend. And so I was floating down the rapids. I was too exhausted to do anything.

20
00:13:11.000 --> 00:13:55.000
And then all of a sudden I heard my name kind of croaked out. And I looked over and there was Peter on the shore of the river. And he waded into the river and he pulled me out of the river and pulled me up on the bank. And then I went into shock. like my heart started beating uncontrollably, I thought I was going to have a heart attack. And he actually has an MD, and he made use of it, and he calmed me down, and he got me over it, got me through the shock.

21
00:13:55.000 --> 00:14:32.000
And so in some ways, this horrific incident happened because of this hedonic treadmill, right? Because at this point, you had actually gotten so used to the excitement of the water, you were looking for something more. and in some ways that's what sent you over the edge of the dam. That's right. It was the kind of the letdown feeling, the feeling of boredom. And for me, boredom is excruciatingly painful. I think a lot of people, a lot of self-destructive things that people do are driven by boredom.

22
00:14:32.000 --> 00:15:12.000
And that incident was like a perfect illustration. So one enemy of happiness that lurks within us is our tendency to get used to things that pleased us or thrilled us at one point in time. A second enemy arises from the way our emotional states temporarily reprogram the mind and body. One element of this dynamic, George, is that when we are sad or fearful, our minds reach for sad and fearful thoughts that amplify our suffering. That's absolutely right. There's a whole lot of different reasons why when we're in a negative emotional state,

23
00:15:12.000 --> 00:16:07.000
we tend to get kind of pinned down into that state. Perversely, when we're in a down state, our brain just naturally and automatically tends to recall negative memories, memories of social blunders, memories of things that we regret, and that also helps to pin us down in a negative emotional state. And then for many emotional states, we often take actions that worsen our situation. And jealousy is a perfect example of that. When people are jealous, they often do things that are either very insecure and clingy, or they do things that are very aggressive that end up producing exactly the effect that they fear.

24
00:16:07.000 --> 00:16:47.000
What you're talking about is feedback loops that very often negative states end up perpetuating themselves via a variety of different mechanisms. So this calls to mind the sad end of the author and the Holocaust survivor Primo Levy. He was the author of Survival at Auschwitz, but there was a foe he confronted later in life that turned out to be in some ways as dangerous as a Nazi concentration camp. Tell me that story, George. Yeah, there's a lot of things. controversy about Primo Levy's death. Some people think it was an accident, but I think most people

25
00:16:47.000 --> 00:17:49.000
do believe it was a suicide. There's also a controversy about what role his time in Auschwitz played in his suicide. So Elie Wiesel famously said that Primo Levy died at Auschwitz 40 years later. He kind of attributed the suicide to Auschwitz. But Ian Thompson, his definitive biographer, had a much more prosaic account of his death, which he did believe was a suicide. So Levy had insomnia. He was in a deep depression, and Thompson said that he just couldn't see his way out. And there's actually a remarkable quote from Thompson's biography from Primo Levy, quote, I know that this phase will pass just as the others have done, but I'm aware of this only at the rational level. My overriding oppression is that it will last

26
00:17:49.000 --> 00:18:29.000
forever and that I will never find an exit. I think when we're in the midst of a depression, it's just hard to see our way out of it. It feels like we always have been depressed, and we always will be depressed. We often imagine, that the enemies of happiness lie outside us, in our circumstances, our setbacks, the difficult people we have to deal with. But many of the forces that erode happiness come from within. When we come back, why our emotions work against us,

27
00:18:29.000 --> 00:19:11.000
nudging us toward the negative, even when we want to feel positive? You're listening to Hidden Brain. I'm Shankar Vedantam. This is Hidden Brain. I'm Shankar Vedanta. George Lewinstein is a psychologist at Carnegie Melle. University. He studies how our emotions shape our judgments. George, in your effort to understand negative emotions, you conducted what you call a misery survey. You asked hundreds of people to tell you about their greatest misery. What did they tell

28
00:19:11.000 --> 00:19:47.000
you? People really bared their soul. It was absolutely remarkable going through their responses. I mean, one of the things I noticed was that a lot of the miseries had, had taken place years or even many decades earlier, and they were still very much alive for the respondents. Another question I asked was, how long did the misery last? And are you still experiencing the misery? And just an amazing number of people reported

29
00:19:47.000 --> 00:21:02.000
that they were still experiencing the misery a decade or many decades after the fact. In some ways, the picture that is painted from this study is that large numbers of people are really leading what Thoreau would call lives of quiet desperation. That's right. At least at a minimum, it seems like almost everyone has pockets of misery. But I do think that there are an awful lot of people who are leading lives of quiet desperation, where the miseries are kind of the dominant force in their lives. in their lives. I mean, there are lots of people who never get into social situations because they have such extreme social phobias. So we've been talking about the internal enemies of happiness, the ways our emotions conspire to undermine any lasting sense of well-being. It strikes me that among the people

30
00:21:02.000 --> 00:21:54.000
who participated in your misery survey, I'm guessing that none of them wanted to feel bad, but they couldn't stop feeling bad. And in some ways, this is both odd and also familiar. So we think we should be able to control negative emotions, but we all also have been up at three o'clock in the morning, worried about something. We want to stop spinning our wheels, but find that we can't stop. Can you talk a moment about how this inability to control our own feelings is another internal enemy of happiness? I mean, it is really remarkable in lots of the different stories that I read from the misery survey, it just seemed patently obvious that it was within the respondent's control to end the misery. Like, for example, there's so many people living with anger. Some of people in their

31
00:21:54.000 --> 00:22:44.000
60s or 70s living with anger that dates back to things that happened to them in their childhood, and they just can't let it go. And resentments towards ex-spouses, towards colleagues. And it would be so beneficial for these people to forgive and forget, and yet they are unable to do that. I have a paper with a former graduate student named Shereen Chaudry, and it's titled, Thanking, Apologizing, Bragging, and Blaming. And it particularly focuses on thanking and apologizing. And in so many cases, a simple thank you or a simple apology would fix rifts and relationships. and yet people are just unable to get themselves to utter the words.

32
00:22:44.000 --> 00:23:27.000
And it's quite analogous to anger and envy. So many of these emotions, if people could just let go, they could move past their misery. So a central idea underlying many facets of how the mind works is that our brains are the product of a long evolutionary process. You and others argue that natural selection has construed. constructed our minds to be survival machines, not happiness machines? Well, survival and reproduction machines.

33
00:23:27.000 --> 00:24:09.000
Reproduction might be even more important than survival. Yes, we're designed for evolutionary fitness. And so talk about this idea. In some ways, I think when we think about our own minds, we say that obviously we want to be happy. Our minds must, in some ways, be designed to help us be happy, but perhaps that's not what our minds are designed to do. Our minds are designed to do things that will enhance our evolutionary fitness, which means help us to survive and reproduce. And happiness and unhappiness are just mechanisms that the brain has evolved to get us to do the things that we need to do to survive and reproduce.

34
00:24:09.000 --> 00:25:03.000
And it turns out that negative emotions are much more effective in getting us to do these things than positive emotions are. And that's probably the reason why there are so many more negative emotions than there are positive emotions. In a lot of cases, there isn't even a positive emotion that corresponds to the negative emotion. If you think of any kind of positive emotion, positive emotions do almost always have negative counterparts. So think about something like pride, and the inverse of pride is shame. It's nice to feel proud, but it's not that strong an emotion, and that it's pretty short-lived. But shame can be extraordinarily miserable. You almost can't plumb the depths of shame.

35
00:25:03.000 --> 00:25:48.000
And shame is very lasting. It can put you in a negative state for weeks, months, years. So there's just no comparison. For every pair of emotions that exist, of positive and negative emotions, the negative emotion is invariably stronger and more lasting than the positive counterpart. And as I said, many negative emotions don't really have a positive counterpart. For example, dread. Dread is a hugely important negative emotion, and we don't even have a word in the English language for the positive counterpart. We have to use

36
00:25:48.000 --> 00:26:43.000
pleasurable anticipation. But there's no word for that. And I think that's very telling. So the psychologist Paul Rosen has captured this dynamic with a very vivid metaphor. He says that if you drop a cockroach into a vat of wine, the whole thing is ruined. But if you pour fine wine on a cockroach, it does nothing to improve the cockroach. So in some ways, this is the roach effect that you're describing here. That's true. That's absolutely true. A nice illustration of that would be Imagine going to dinner with your significant other, and you go to a really, really nice restaurant, the best food imaginable. And then the waiter comes over and behaves in a kind of a high-handed fashion. That's going to ruin the food.

37
00:26:43.000 --> 00:27:21.000
All it takes is one cockroach to kind of take the pleasure out of one's activities. The psychologist Mark Leary says we all carry around what he calls a sociometer. What is this, George? How does this affect us? Self-esteem is a nice illustration of the asymmetry between the negative and the positive because you can feel high self-esteem and that's good, but there's no comparison to the depths of low self-esteem that are possible. In any case, Leary argues that,

38
00:27:21.000 --> 00:28:21.000
But when we were doing our evolving, much of which we actually did before humans existed, we lived in groups. And it was very, very important for our survival and reproduction to remain part of the group. And being cast out of the group was virtually a death sentence. And when something is so important, we tend to evolve psychological mechanisms to have. help us deal with those situations. And Leary postulates that we develop this sociometer. And it's constantly monitoring our world. It's doing it automatically. It's always turned on. And to see how we're being valued by the people around us. That's the sociometer. And that's what we commonly refer to as self-esteem. And I think one of his profound insights,

39
00:28:21.000 --> 00:29:14.000
is sometimes we think that self-esteem is an internal matter, that if only we could develop a high internal sense of self-esteem that didn't depend on how other people view us. But according to sociometer theory, the idea of how other people view us is inextricable from self-esteem and vice versa. That self-esteem is a measure of how we think the world is evaluating and valuing us. So we've been talking about how sensitive we are to the judgments of others, but in some ways our own judgments about ourselves can also be very harsh. One of the things that you are self-critical about has to do with your willingness to spend

40
00:29:14.000 --> 00:29:44.000
money. You dislike overspending, but you also dislike feeling cheap? That's right. I've done a lot of research on individual differences in spending behavior. And there are some people who are spend thrifts. They spend easily. But there's other people and actually a lot more of these other people who are tightwads. They find it painful to spend money.

41
00:29:44.000 --> 00:30:33.000
And as a result, they don't spend money. I'm in the tightwad category. I just came back from a trip trekking in Peru with a bunch of of male friends who all happen to be tightwads as well. But one of our number is like a ridiculously wealthy individual. He's like a real estate developer and his children and his nieces and nephews, they all have trust funds. But on this trip, I noticed, as I've often noticed on our outdoor trips, his boots were falling apart. And his backpack was held together with a paperclip.

42
00:30:33.000 --> 00:31:02.000
And he's just, he's just completely unwilling and unable to spend money. And he knows he's ridiculous. But there's nothing he can do about it. So that's what I find interesting. It's not so much that you actually are a tight wad, but that you know that you're a tight wad and you want to stop and you find that you're not able to stop. I understand you experienced this dilemma during a trip to Croatia at one point in your life?

43
00:31:02.000 --> 00:31:55.000
That's right, and it was the same individual, and we were going to sea kayaking the next day, and we were staying in a hotel in a town called Split, and we just got a single room, and with a single moderately large bed, and we found ourselves sharing a bed, and Croatia at that time was, pretty inexpensive, and we both have plenty of money. And I was having trouble sleeping, just because I didn't really want to be sharing a bed with my friend. And I just thought to myself, this is insanity. But I mean, I think being a tightwad, it's part of a more general pattern that we tend to be kind of victims of ourselves. We're trapped.

44
00:31:55.000 --> 00:32:29.000
and our own personalities. Our emotions can undermine our happiness in powerful and surprising ways. Is there anything that can be done about their hold on our minds? That's when we come back. You're listening to Hidden Brain. I'm Shankar Vedantam.

