00:33:11.000 --> 00:34:07.000
George Lewinstein is a psychologist at Carnegie Mellon University. He's the author of The Opposite of Happiness, How Bad Feelings Make and Break Us. George, in your book you write about the psychologist John Gottman and his discovery that we often take one of two attitudes when someone comes to us with their emotions. Either we are accepting or we are dismissive of their emotions. Talk about these two attitudes and your own insight about how we respond to our own emotions. Gottman talks about how we respond to our own emotions. parents, caretakers, and friends and partners can be accepting or dismissing of their partners or their children's emotions. And he distinguishes between two different stances that people

48
00:34:07.000 --> 00:35:07.000
can take and accepting a dismissive stance. And he says that a dismissive stance, kind of denying other people's emotions, downplaying them, belittling them, tends to exacerbate those emotions and their negative consequences. And I take Gottman's work in the book, and I say that we often play this out in our own minds. We take an accepting or a dismissive stance towards our own emotions. These are emotions about emotions. So let's say that we experience envy towards a friend and we think that that's unacceptable. Then we feel guilt that we were experiencing envy towards our friend. Or suppose that a relative dies and we don't experience grief, then we feel guilt or shame

49
00:35:07.000 --> 00:35:40.000
because we didn't experience grief. These are all meta-emotions. They're kind of like emotions squared. It's like the emotions aren't bad enough. We have to flagellate ourselves with the feeling that we aren't experiencing the right emotions, the emotions that we should be experiencing. So Gottman finds that people who respond to their partners' emotions in a more accepting manner. These people have happier relationships, their partners are better off.

50
00:35:40.000 --> 00:36:08.000
It's a healthier way of responding to our partners to be accepting to what their emotions, rather than dismissive. I'm assuming the same thing must hold true of us. In some ways, does accepting our own emotions, can that ameliorate some of the sting of negative emotions, George? I think that is true to some extent, because, as I said, these meta-emotions are kind of emotion squared. And when we judge ourselves negatively for the emotions

51
00:36:08.000 --> 00:36:43.000
that we are experiencing or are not experiencing, that just worsens the emotions. and often introduces a whole new mix of negative emotions. I think one of the reasons why we do tend to self-flagellate is because we think that our emotions are more unique, more unusual than they actually are. And I do think that it can be useful to people to appreciate how widespread the negative emotions

52
00:36:43.000 --> 00:37:13.000
that they are experiencing are, and then they're less likely to blame themselves for feeling those emotions. It strikes me that as we are reading a novel, a well-written novel, you know, even though the characters may be experiencing all kinds of negative emotions or making all kinds of mistakes,

53
00:37:13.000 --> 00:37:31.000
very often, you know, we don't sit in judgment of those characters. We have a lot of empathy for them, a beautifully written novel, even when people do things, things that are really foolhardy or really dangerous to themselves or others, we end up feeling compassion for them. And it's so striking that we're able to feel compassion for other people's emotions when they're

54
00:37:31.000 --> 00:38:27.000
presented in a novelistic form, when we often have very little compassion for our own emotions, even though we're experiencing them just as vividly as reading a novel. That's right. I think you're absolutely right that that's one of the functions that novels serve. It does make us more compassionate towards people. Tolstoy famously was very, very negatively judgmental towards Anna Karenina, a character who he developed. But as he wrote the book, he became more and more sympathetic to her, which I think is part of the beauty of the novel. She's not really that nicer character, but the sympathy for her comes through.

55
00:38:27.000 --> 00:38:58.000
So another thing that we can do with our negative emotions is to discuss them with others and thereby discover how universal negative emotions are. Talk a moment about why this helps. Why does it help to talk about our negative emotions? I have a paper with Cass Sunstein and Aaron Carbone. It's titled Spirals of Silence. And it's about how when people are embarrassed or ashamed of something, they don't talk about it.

56
00:38:58.000 --> 00:39:44.000
And when people don't talk about it, you don't hear about it, and you think it's rare. And then when you yourself are experiencing it, the fact that it's so rare makes you embarrassed and you ashamed of it, and then you don't talk about it. And it creates what we call a spiral of shame, a spiral of silence. And so I think you're actually doing a public good by sharing your, negative emotions. I understand that you found some relief in hearing someone else confessed to a particular shortcoming that you thought that you suffered alone?

57
00:39:44.000 --> 00:40:26.000
Yes. This story is about how when I was an adolescent and young teenager, I suffered from this problem, I had a difficulty urinating in public places. And I never heard about this issue. It turns out to be extremely widespread, but I didn't know that. And I wondered what it meant about me. I thought, like, maybe, well, I had all sorts of negative ideas about what it might mean. And eventually, I actually just naturally got over it.

58
00:40:26.000 --> 00:40:50.000
But years after I got over it, I was in graduate school. and I was at a dinner party and a close friend just casually made a reference to his own stage fright as he referred to it and it was perfectly obvious what he was referring to and I was just shocked when he talked about it

59
00:40:50.000 --> 00:41:18.000
because it's something that I had just kept hidden for decades and he just mentioned it so casually and nobody blinked an eye It really brought home how you mentioned the rose, like, what is it, lives of... Lives of quiet desperation. Quiet desperation, yeah. How many of us are living with these shameful secrets, which if we went public about them,

60
00:41:18.000 --> 00:41:57.000
nobody would blink an eye, nobody would judge us for, and if we shared them, we might stop judging ourselves for them. As we've talked about, George, one of the factors that makes it so hard to stay happy in a lasting way is that positive feelings tend to fade over time. And that's true and that's sad. But of course, the same is also true for negative feelings. You write that misery isn't built to last? That's true. Adaptation is a two-edged sword. On the one hand, it robs us of lasting happiness.

61
00:41:57.000 --> 00:42:39.000
But on the other hand, it is also merciful. but it's not universal. There are some things that people don't adapt to, loss of a child, sometimes, divorce, unemployment. There are things that cause very lasting misery. So, yes, adaptation is a two-edged sword, and it is merciful because it means that pain is not necessarily lasting. But there are things that can interfere with our adaptation to be. pain to misery. A reminder, has this conversation led you to think about your own experience with negative

62
